WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.468
Welcome to Lecture 13 of the

2
00:00:02.545 --> 00:00:05.100
FrontierCS 2 course, on robotics.

3
00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:07.865
FrontierCS 2 takes a research paper

4
00:00:07.934 --> 00:00:09.728
and the authors' own code

5
00:00:09.728 --> 00:00:11.799
usually removes its key idea

6
00:00:11.799 --> 00:00:14.490
and asks an AI agent to write it back.

7
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.647
A run passes only if it does at least as well

8
00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:20.018
as the authors' code on every metric

9
00:00:20.018 --> 00:00:21.640
on every hidden workload.

10
00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.390
This last lecture has two tasks.

11
00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:27.078
Diffusion Policy samples a robot's

12
00:00:27.146 --> 00:00:29.140
next moves by removing noise.

13
00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:32.120
MuJoCo Playground trains a four-legged

14
00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:34.750
robot to follow a joystick in minutes

15
00:00:34.819 --> 00:00:35.650
of GPU time.

16
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.240
We start with Diffusion Policy.

17
00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.228
A robot policy maps what the robot

18
00:00:41.299 --> 00:00:43.140
sees to what it does next.

19
00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:45.754
The usual way to learn one from human

20
00:00:45.815 --> 00:00:47.589
demonstrations is regression

21
00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:49.608
predict the demonstrated action

22
00:00:49.608 --> 00:00:51.810
with the squared error as the loss.

23
00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.920
Here is a toy of our own.

24
00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.190
The action is one number.

25
00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:58.843
An obstacle stands straight ahead

26
00:00:58.843 --> 00:01:01.485
and the demonstrators steer around it

27
00:01:01.485 --> 00:01:04.263
to the left, minus one, or to the right

28
00:01:04.263 --> 00:01:04.940
plus one.

29
00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:07.423
Regression learns the average of

30
00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.290
two hundred demonstrations

31
00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:10.890
zero point one one two.

32
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:12.837
That steers almost straight

33
00:01:12.896 --> 00:01:13.960
into the obstacle.

34
00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.487
Not one demonstration lies within

35
00:01:16.553 --> 00:01:18.070
zero point three of it.

36
00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:20.991
Diffusion Policy, from RSS 2023

37
00:01:21.064 --> 00:01:23.552
learns to generate actions the way

38
00:01:23.625 --> 00:01:25.893
image models generate pictures

39
00:01:25.893 --> 00:01:27.722
start from random noise

40
00:01:27.722 --> 00:01:29.770
and remove it step by step.

41
00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.936
Training adds noise to the demonstrated

42
00:01:33.008 --> 00:01:35.679
actions at many levels; on our scale

43
00:01:35.679 --> 00:01:38.134
from zero, clean, to one hundred

44
00:01:38.134 --> 00:01:39.000
pure noise.

45
00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.464
A cosine schedule sets how much

46
00:01:41.532 --> 00:01:43.510
signal is left at each level.

47
00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.928
We skip the training and use the best

48
00:01:45.984 --> 00:01:47.940
possible guess of the clean action.

49
00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:49.986
Start from a noisy action

50
00:01:50.054 --> 00:01:51.410
of zero point three.

51
00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.470
At level one hundred, no signal is left

52
00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:56.367
and the best guess is zero

53
00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:58.400
the average of the two modes.

54
00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:02.350
At sixty, the guess is zero point two six.

55
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.500
At twenty, zero point nine three.

56
00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:07.103
As the noise falls

57
00:02:07.103 --> 00:02:09.610
the guess commits to the closer mode.

58
00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:11.790
A sampler uses this.

59
00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.979
It starts from pure noise, asks for a guess

60
00:02:14.979 --> 00:02:17.367
moves the sample a little toward it

61
00:02:17.367 --> 00:02:19.432
adds back a little fresh noise

62
00:02:19.432 --> 00:02:21.820
and repeats at the next lower level.

63
00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.410
This is the DDPM sampler.

64
00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:26.307
The authors' loop takes

65
00:02:26.374 --> 00:02:27.853
all one hundred steps

66
00:02:27.853 --> 00:02:29.400
one network call each.

67
00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:32.230
We drew one thousand actions this way.

68
00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.941
Four hundred seventy-eight go left

69
00:02:34.941 --> 00:02:37.234
five hundred twenty-two go right

70
00:02:37.234 --> 00:02:39.460
and none goes into the obstacle.

71
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.600
But a hundred network calls are slow.

72
00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:44.033
On the task's CPU

73
00:02:44.094 --> 00:02:46.682
the authors' sampler takes one thousand one

74
00:02:46.742 --> 00:02:49.270
hundred fifty milliseconds per prediction.

75
00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.900
The DDIM update drops the fresh noise.

76
00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.648
It splits the sample into a clean guess

77
00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:57.739
and the noise that guess implies

78
00:02:57.739 --> 00:03:00.199
then rebuilds it at a much lower level

79
00:03:00.199 --> 00:03:01.490
with the same noise.

80
00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.312
Spread the calls evenly

81
00:03:03.312 --> 00:03:05.520
and count the misses in the middle.

82
00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:08.473
With one call, all one thousand miss

83
00:03:08.544 --> 00:03:10.162
one guess at full noise

84
00:03:10.232 --> 00:03:11.710
is the average again.

85
00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.700
Three calls miss two hundred fifty-eight.

86
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:16.810
Five miss fifty-three.

87
00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.412
Ten miss only two

88
00:03:18.412 --> 00:03:20.360
about as good as a hundred.

89
00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.540
Pause and predict.

90
00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.698
Three even calls, at levels one hundred

91
00:03:25.698 --> 00:03:27.803
sixty-seven and thirty-three

92
00:03:27.803 --> 00:03:30.330
miss two hundred fifty-eight times.

93
00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.801
Will calls at ninety-five

94
00:03:32.801 --> 00:03:34.840
fifty and ten do better?

95
00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:40.950
Much better: forty-four misses.

96
00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.316
Even spacing wastes a call at the

97
00:03:43.377 --> 00:03:45.026
top of the cosine schedule

98
00:03:45.026 --> 00:03:46.980
where almost no signal is left.

99
00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:49.142
With five calls

100
00:03:49.142 --> 00:03:51.983
placement alone gains nothing in our toy

101
00:03:51.983 --> 00:03:54.620
fifty-four misses against fifty-three.

102
00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:56.435
A second-order step

103
00:03:56.435 --> 00:03:58.560
called DPM-Solver plus plus

104
00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:00.905
also reuses the previous guess

105
00:04:00.905 --> 00:04:02.810
at no extra network call.

106
00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:06.333
At levels ninety-five, seventy, forty-five

107
00:04:06.333 --> 00:04:09.506
twenty-two and five, it halves the misses

108
00:04:09.506 --> 00:04:10.760
to twenty-three.

109
00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:13.763
So: regression averages two good

110
00:04:13.839 --> 00:04:15.580
actions into a bad one.

111
00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:19.050
At full noise, the best guess is the mean.

112
00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.128
And a sampler's time is its

113
00:04:21.192 --> 00:04:23.816
number of network calls: place them well

114
00:04:23.816 --> 00:04:25.480
and reuse the last guess.

115
00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:26.776
As an exercise

116
00:04:26.839 --> 00:04:29.298
make the demonstrations seventy percent

117
00:04:29.361 --> 00:04:31.252
left and thirty percent right

118
00:04:31.252 --> 00:04:33.270
and see how the sampler splits.

119
00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:35.780
Next: the sampling task.
